# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values. Never commit your real .env.
# The verify recipe and deploy notes are in docs/CONVENTIONS.md.

# --- Required ---

# PostgreSQL connection string.
# Local dev (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md for the one-time role/db setup):
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://app:app@localhost:5432/opip"
# (Mac with Homebrew Postgres: "postgresql://<your-mac-username>@localhost:5432/opip", no password.)

# The tests' own database, kept apart from the development data above.
# Optional — when unset, `npm test` uses DATABASE_URL with "_test" added to the
# database name (here: opip_test), creating and migrating it automatically
# with `prisma migrate deploy`. Must NOT be the same as DATABASE_URL.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://app:app@localhost:5432/opip_test"

# Auth.js session secret. Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="change-me-to-a-long-random-string"

# The app's own public URL (used by Auth.js). Local dev value:
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Where uploaded property images are written. On Railway this is the mounted
# volume path (DATA_DIR=/data); without a volume, photos are wiped every deploy.
DATA_DIR="./.data"

# Password for the seeded admin login (admin@example.com) created by `npm run db:seed`.
# The same password seeds the sample agency login (agency@example.com).
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD="change-me"

# --- Sign-up gate (invitation-only until payments exist) ---

# Comma-separated invite codes, each 8+ characters (shorter ones are ignored).
# When set, both sign-up forms (register and list-with-us) demand one of these
# codes. Rotate a code by editing this variable and redeploying. In production
# with NO codes and SIGNUPS_OPEN unset, sign-up is CLOSED to everyone. Locally
# (development/test) sign-up stays open unless codes are set.
# SIGNUP_INVITE_CODES="muscat-2026-alpha,salalah-2026-beta"

# "true" opens sign-up to everyone (overrides the codes). Leave unset for now.
# SIGNUPS_OPEN="true"

# --- Legal pages ---

# Contact address shown on the Privacy Policy (/privacy) and Terms of Use
# (/terms) pages as a mailto link. Optional — when unset it defaults to the
# owner's own address (naeljam@hotmail.com). Logic: src/lib/legalContact.ts
# PRIVACY_CONTACT_EMAIL="privacy@your-domain.om"

# --- AI analyst (Phase 4) ---

# Anthropic API key that powers the AI analyst on property pages. Without it the
# analyst card shows a friendly "not switched on yet" message and everything
# else works. Get one at https://console.anthropic.com
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# --- Daily job: averages from live listings ---

# Shared secret for the scheduled job at POST /api/jobs/listing-averages (the
# scheduler sends it as "Authorization: Bearer <secret>"). At least 16
# characters; with it unset or shorter the job address refuses everyone.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Railway: set it, then add a cron schedule that POSTs to the job daily
# ("0 23 * * *" = 03:00 Oman time). The admin "Recompute now" button works
# without it.
# The same secret guards the HOURLY saved-search alert job,
# POST /api/jobs/saved-search-alerts (cron "0 * * * *"):
#   curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" "$AUTH_URL/api/jobs/saved-search-alerts"
# CRON_SECRET=""

# --- Email (off until both are set) ---

# Sending happens through Resend's HTTP API (src/lib/email/send.ts). Leave
# either of these blank and the app sends nothing at all — no errors, no
# broken pages. Saved-search alert emails are sent by the hourly alert job
# above; see GO-LIVE.md ("Alert job").
# RESEND_API_KEY=""
# The "from" address on anything we send. The domain must be verified in
# Resend first, e.g. "Oman Property Intelligence <alerts@yourdomain.com>".
# EMAIL_FROM=""
# LOCAL TESTING ONLY: "true" writes each alert email as a .eml file in
# DATA_DIR/outbox/ instead of sending it. Ignored in production.
# EMAIL_DEV_LOG="true"

# --- Phone codes for private owners (off until all three are set) ---

# Twilio Verify texts a 6-digit code to a private owner's Omani mobile
# (src/lib/sms/send.ts). Leave any of the three blank and: in development the
# site makes its own code and prints it in the server console; in production
# phone verification (and so owner listing) is CLOSED with a clear message.
# Create a Twilio account, then Verify -> Services -> Create new, and copy:
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=""
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=""
# TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=""

# --- Featured-listing card payments (Thawani Pay; off until BOTH keys are set) ---

# Owners and agencies can pay to feature a live listing (src/lib/payments/).
# Leave either key blank and the site says "online payments aren't switched on
# yet — contact us"; nothing is created and Thawani is never called. Get the
# keys from merchant.thawani.om. Thawani gives separate SANDBOX (test money)
# and PRODUCTION keys: start with the sandbox. The secret key is read on the
# server only and is never shown or logged. AUTH_URL must be the live https
# address (Thawani sends the customer back to it).
# THAWANI_API_KEY=""
# THAWANI_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=""
# "sandbox" (default, test money) or "production" (real money)
# THAWANI_ENV="sandbox"

# --- Optional ---

# Map tile server. The default OpenStreetMap tiles are fine for low traffic /
# local dev only — swap to a Carto/Protomaps URL before real production traffic.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL="https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
# Production map: ONE Protomaps file (.pmtiles) on Cloudflare R2. When set it
# wins over NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL; unset = the tile URL above is used.
# Build-time variable (redeploy after changing). See GO-LIVE.md.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_PMTILES_URL="https://tiles.example.com/oman.pmtiles"
# Optional: attribution text for the plain tile URL above (PMTiles shows its own).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_ATTRIBUTION="© OpenStreetMap contributors"

# Cloudflare Turnstile — a free CAPTCHA ("verify you're human" box) on the
# login, register and list-with-us forms. DORMANT until BOTH keys are set: no
# box is shown and nothing is verified. Get the pair from the Cloudflare
# dashboard (Turnstile → Add site). The site key is public (it ships to the
# browser); the secret key never leaves the server.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=""
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=""

# Visitor stats — a privacy-friendly page-view counter (Plausible, Umami or
# GoatCounter; no cookies, no consent banner). DORMANT until BOTH the script
# address (must be https://) and the site id are set: no script is added and
# the security policy is unchanged. All three values are public-safe. Read at
# build time, so redeploy after changing them.
#   Plausible:  script https://plausible.io/js/script.js, site id = your domain, attr data-domain
#   Umami:      script from your Umami site, site id = website id, attr data-website-id (default)
#   GoatCounter: script https://gc.zgo.at/count.js, site id = https://NAME.goatcounter.com/count, attr data-goatcounter
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STATS_SCRIPT_URL=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STATS_SITE_ID=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STATS_ATTR=""

# Error tracking (Sentry). Leave empty = OFF (dormant): nothing is sent and the
# SDK never initializes. Set it to your Sentry project DSN to switch on alerts.
# SENTRY_DSN=""

# Shared rate-limit store (scaling seam). Leave empty: a fast in-memory limiter
# is used, so limits count PER SERVER PROCESS. Setting this is the future switch
# for a shared Redis-backed limiter across multiple instances.
# REDIS_URL=""

# Trust the X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-Ip headers for per-IP rate limiting
# (login, signup, etc). These headers are visitor-supplied and easy to spoof
# UNLESS a reverse proxy in front of the app overwrites them with the real
# connecting IP before your app ever sees the request. Set this to "true"
# only when that's the case — e.g. on Railway, whose edge network sets these
# headers itself and cannot be bypassed by the visitor. Leave unset/blank for
# local dev or any setup where the app is reachable directly.
#
# When this is OFF (the default), the app cannot tell visitors apart by
# forwarded IP headers, so anonymous rate limiting (login/signup/enquiries)
# falls back to a stable id in a signed, httpOnly cookie per browser instead
# — see getAnonRateLimitKey in src/lib/rate-limit.ts. A cookie-less caller
# (a script that never accepts cookies) is bounded by the real TCP socket
# address instead (stamped onto every request by src/instrumentation-node.ts
# — see SOCKET_IP_HEADER in src/lib/clientIp.ts), never a single shared
# bucket. Without a trusted proxy that address is whatever machine is
# actually connecting — if this app IS behind a proxy you don't control for,
# that's the proxy's own address for every visitor, which is why this is
# still weaker than a real per-IP limit. The app logs a warning on first use
# in production if this stays off — set it to "true" on Railway before going
# live.
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS="true"

# How many trusted reverse proxies sit between the visitor and the app. Only
# used when TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS is "true". The visitor's address is read as the
# Nth entry from the RIGHT of X-Forwarded-For (everything to its left is
# visitor-supplied and can be forged). Default 1 (e.g. Railway's edge alone).
# Set this to match the real proxy chain; change it if a CDN is added in front
# (e.g. Cloudflare + Railway = 2). 0 = ignore the header completely. Must be a
# whole number 0-10; anything else stops the server at startup.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
